    {
      "id": "659BqxQAYrarwyVoBqkbS",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -37.10766190873028,
      "y": 554.3559887860475,
      "width": 936.2994995117188,
      "height": 875,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0U",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 724085317,
      "version": 2470,
      "versionNonce": 482411557,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1791550367962,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "NOTES\n\nMAPPING:\n- Gives each thread its element: (row, col).\n- Uses the global index formula, once for x and once for y.\n- x is the column, y is the row.\n- Happens once per thread.\n- Mapping is needed because the grid and block size may not line up\nperfectly with the matrix dimensions.\n- Because the grid rounds up, mapping hands some threads (row, col) that's outside the matrix;\nthose threads do nothing, so we have after mapping checks:\ncol < width AND row < height.\n\nLINEARIZING:\n- Memory has no rows. It only understands one position number, like \"slot 6\".\n- Linearizing turns (row, col) into that number, so the thread can read or write.\n- Uses the strides: row * (row stride) + col * (col stride),\nrow-major: row * N + col\n- The matrix is already stored flat; we didn't flatten it; we find our spot in it.\n- Happens every time a thread touches memory.\n\nHOW THEY RELATE:\n- Two steps, always in this order:\nmapping -> (row, col) -> linearizing -> memory index\n- Both formulas \"multiply then add\", but they count different things:\nMapping counts THREADS (blocks before mine + positon)\nLinearizing counts MEMORY SLOTS (rows before mine + my position)\n- Stage 0: both collapsed into index_1d. The data was already flat,\nso seat = element = memory index.\n\nSPEED:\n- Neighbouring threads (next x) read at the same time, and memory\nhands over neighbouring bytes in on go.\n- x -> column + row major data = neighbour threads read neighbouring memory = fast.\n- Following the convention gets this automatically.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "NOTES\n\nMAPPING:\n- Gives each thread its element: (row, col).\n- Uses the global index formula, once for x and once for y.\n- x is the column, y is the row.\n- Happens once per thread.\n- Mapping is needed because the grid and block size may not line up\nperfectly with the matrix dimensions.\n- Because the grid rounds up, mapping hands some threads (row, col) that's outside the matrix;\nthose threads do nothing, so we have after mapping checks:\ncol < width AND row < height.\n\nLINEARIZING:\n- Memory has no rows. It only understands one position number, like \"slot 6\".\n- Linearizing turns (row, col) into that number, so the thread can read or write.\n- Uses the strides: row * (row stride) + col * (col stride),\nrow-major: row * N + col\n- The matrix is already stored flat; we didn't flatten it; we find our spot in it.\n- Happens every time a thread touches memory.\n\nHOW THEY RELATE:\n- Two steps, always in this order:\nmapping -> (row, col) -> linearizing -> memory index\n- Both formulas \"multiply then add\", but they count different things:\nMapping counts THREADS (blocks before mine + positon)\nLinearizing counts MEMORY SLOTS (rows before mine + my position)\n- Stage 0: both collapsed into index_1d. The data was already flat,\nso seat = element = memory index.\n\nSPEED:\n- Neighbouring threads (next x) read at the same time, and memory\nhands over neighbouring bytes in on go.\n- x -> column + row major data = neighbour threads read neighbouring memory = fast.\n- Following the convention gets this automatically.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
